UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 7, Lesson 1: Reading simple notation', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in treble clef. Letter names (such as C D E F G) help you read a short melody. Start by naming each note, then clap the rhythm, then play or sing. Keep eyes moving ahead of the note you are playing.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA staff shows E on a line, then F in the next space up. Is the second note higher or lower, and what should you do before playing?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing.\n\nWATCH FOR\nGuessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCopy a four-note melody onto staff paper (or lined paper as a staff). Label letter names, clap it and perform it. Write one tricky spot and how you fixed it.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in treble clef. Letter names (such as C D E F G) help you read a short melody. Start by naming each note, then clap the rhythm, then play or sing. Keep eyes moving ahead of the note you are playing.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Copy a four-note melody onto staff paper (or lined paper as a staff). Label letter names, clap it and perform it. Write one tricky spot and how you fixed it. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing. Reasoning: I used notation by attending to staff rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. Correction: Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing. Why: the correction restores accurate use of notation.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating notation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with notation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing. This matches the lesson skill for notation.","options":["Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing.","Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"A staff shows E on a line, then F in the next space up. Is the second note higher or lower, and what should you do before playing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in…","Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing.","Careful use of staff with clear evidence.","Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named staff, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the staff used."],"question":"A learner is practising notation. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing. uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing.","Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about notation."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about staff?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on xylophone bars…","Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space;… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first.","Copy a four-note melody onto staff paper (or lined paper as a staff). Label letter names, clap it and perform it. Write one tricky spot and how you…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating notation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing. Reasoning: I used notation by attending to staff rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with notation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. Correction: Strong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing. Why: the correction restores accurate use of notation.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w07-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise notation.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use notation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify staff and explain why it matters.","I can practise note head independently on a new prompt.","I can notice pitch name needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in treble clef. Letter names (such as C D E F G) help you read a short melody. Start by naming each note, then clap the…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in treble clef. Letter names (such as C D E F G) help you read a short melody. Start by naming each note, then clap the rhythm, then play or sing. Keep eyes moving ahead of the note you are playing.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on xylophone bars labelled the same way.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA staff shows E on a line, then F in the next space up. Is the second note higher or lower, and what should you do before playing?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing.","explain":"Simple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in treble clef. Letter names (such as C D E F G) help you read a short melody. Start by naming each note, then clap the rhythm, then play or sing. Keep eyes moving ahead of the note you are playing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Reading the staff.","caption":"Higher usually higher pitch.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Staff</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five lines</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Note</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">line or space</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on x Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on x Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on xylophone bars labelled the same way. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Notation practice order.","caption":"Name, clap, play.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Name</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">letter first</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Then clap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">then play</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGuessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on xylophone bars labelled the same way.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Higher. Name the notes, clap the rhythm, then play slowly.'' Reading order prevents guessing.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Guessing pitches by how the notes look without checking line/space names. Accurate naming comes first. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about notation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSimple notation shows pitch and rhythm on a staff of five lines. Note heads sit on a line or in a space; higher on the staff usually means higher pitch in treble clef. Letter names (such as C D E F G) help you read a short melody. Start by naming each note, then clap the rhythm, then play or sing. Keep eyes moving ahead of the note you are playing. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 7, Lesson 1: Reading simple notation","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Melody, notation and ensemble","week":7,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a four-beat melody on the staff shows G–A–B–G. Pat reads each letter, claps four even beats, then plays it on xylophone bars labelled the same way.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Reading the staff.","caption":"Higher usually higher pitch.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Staff</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five lines</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Note</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">line or space</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Notation practice order.","caption":"Name, clap, play.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" 